{
  "path": "01-forces-and-motion/11-newtons-second-law",
  "title": "Newton's Second Law",
  "pageType": "lesson",
  "content": {
    "type": "doc",
    "content": [
      {
        "type": "heading",
        "attrs": {
          "level": 1
        },
        "content": [
          {
            "type": "text",
            "text": "Newton's Second Law"
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "type": "heading",
        "attrs": {
          "level": 2
        },
        "content": [
          {
            "type": "text",
            "text": "Introduction"
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "type": "paragraph",
        "content": [
          {
            "type": "text",
            "text": "Newton's Second Law is the cornerstone of mechanics. It provides the mathematical relationship between force, mass, and acceleration, allowing us to calculate how objects respond to forces. This is one of the most frequently used equations in physics..."
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "type": "heading",
        "attrs": {
          "level": 2
        },
        "content": [
          {
            "type": "text",
            "text": "The Law"
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "type": "heading",
        "attrs": {
          "level": 3
        },
        "content": [
          {
            "type": "text",
            "text": "Newton's Second Law States:"
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "type": "blockquote",
        "content": [
          {
            "type": "paragraph",
            "content": [
              {
                "type": "text",
                "text": "The acceleration of an object is directly proportional to the resultant force acting on it and inversely proportional to its mass. The acceleration is in the direction of the resultant force."
              }
            ]
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "type": "heading",
        "attrs": {
          "level": 3
        },
        "content": [
          {
            "type": "text",
            "text": "The Equation"
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "type": "paragraph",
        "content": [
          {
            "type": "text",
            "text": "$$F = ma$$"
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "type": "paragraph",
        "content": [
          {
            "type": "text",
            "text": "Or more precisely:"
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "type": "paragraph",
        "content": [
          {
            "type": "text",
            "text": "$$\\vec{F} = m\\vec{a}$$"
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "type": "paragraph",
        "content": [
          {
            "type": "text",
            "text": "Where:"
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "type": "bulletList",
        "content": [
          {
            "type": "listItem",
            "content": [
              {
                "type": "paragraph",
                "content": [
                  {
                    "type": "text",
                    "text": "$F$"
                  },
                  {
                    "type": "text",
                    "text": " = resultant (net) force (N)"
                  }
                ]
              }
            ]
          },
          {
            "type": "listItem",
            "content": [
              {
                "type": "paragraph",
                "content": [
                  {
                    "type": "text",
                    "text": "$m$"
                  },
                  {
                    "type": "text",
                    "text": " = mass (kg)"
                  }
                ]
              }
            ]
          },
          {
            "type": "listItem",
            "content": [
              {
                "type": "paragraph",
                "content": [
                  {
                    "type": "text",
                    "text": "$a$"
                  },
                  {
                    "type": "text",
                    "text": " = acceleration (m/s²)"
                  }
                ]
              }
            ]
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "type": "blockquote",
        "content": [
          {
            "type": "paragraph",
            "content": [
              {
                "type": "text",
                "marks": [
                  {
                    "type": "bold"
                  }
                ],
                "text": "Key Concept: "
              },
              {
                "type": "text",
                "text": "F = ma is one of the most important equations in all of physics. It links force, mass, and acceleration. The force in this equation must be the RESULTANT (net) force, not just any single force."
              }
            ]
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "type": "heading",
        "attrs": {
          "level": 2
        },
        "content": [
          {
            "type": "text",
            "text": "Key Concepts"
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "type": "heading",
        "attrs": {
          "level": 3
        },
        "content": [
          {
            "type": "text",
            "text": "Understanding the Relationships"
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "type": "paragraph",
        "content": [
          {
            "type": "text",
            "marks": [
              {
                "type": "bold"
              }
            ],
            "text": "Direct proportionality to force:"
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "type": "bulletList",
        "content": [
          {
            "type": "listItem",
            "content": [
              {
                "type": "paragraph",
                "content": [
                  {
                    "type": "text",
                    "text": "If you double the force (keeping mass constant), acceleration doubles"
                  }
                ]
              }
            ]
          },
          {
            "type": "listItem",
            "content": [
              {
                "type": "paragraph",
                "content": [
                  {
                    "type": "text",
                    "text": "More force → more acceleration"
                  }
                ]
              }
            ]
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "type": "paragraph",
        "content": [
          {
            "type": "text",
            "marks": [
              {
                "type": "bold"
              }
            ],
            "text": "Inverse proportionality to mass:"
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "type": "bulletList",
        "content": [
          {
            "type": "listItem",
            "content": [
              {
                "type": "paragraph",
                "content": [
                  {
                    "type": "text",
                    "text": "If you double the mass (keeping force constant), acceleration halves"
                  }
                ]
              }
            ]
          },
          {
            "type": "listItem",
            "content": [
              {
                "type": "paragraph",
                "content": [
                  {
                    "type": "text",
                    "text": "More mass → less acceleration (for the same force)"
                  }
                ]
              }
            ]
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "type": "heading",
        "attrs": {
          "level": 3
        },
        "content": [
          {
            "type": "text",
            "text": "The Direction of Acceleration"
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "type": "paragraph",
        "content": [
          {
            "type": "text",
            "text": "Acceleration always occurs in the "
          },
          {
            "type": "text",
            "marks": [
              {
                "type": "bold"
              }
            ],
            "text": "same direction as the resultant force"
          },
          {
            "type": "text",
            "text": "."
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "type": "bulletList",
        "content": [
          {
            "type": "listItem",
            "content": [
              {
                "type": "paragraph",
                "content": [
                  {
                    "type": "text",
                    "text": "Push right → accelerate right"
                  }
                ]
              }
            ]
          },
          {
            "type": "listItem",
            "content": [
              {
                "type": "paragraph",
                "content": [
                  {
                    "type": "text",
                    "text": "Net force upward → accelerate upward"
                  }
                ]
              }
            ]
          },
          {
            "type": "listItem",
            "content": [
              {
                "type": "paragraph",
                "content": [
                  {
                    "type": "text",
                    "text": "If forces balance → no acceleration (First Law)"
                  }
                ]
              }
            ]
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "type": "heading",
        "attrs": {
          "level": 3
        },
        "content": [
          {
            "type": "text",
            "text": "Resultant Force"
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "type": "paragraph",
        "content": [
          {
            "type": "text",
            "text": "In F = ma, "
          },
          {
            "type": "text",
            "marks": [
              {
                "type": "bold"
              }
            ],
            "text": "F is always the resultant force"
          },
          {
            "type": "text",
            "text": ", not any individual force."
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "type": "paragraph",
        "content": [
          {
            "type": "text",
            "text": "To find the resultant:"
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "type": "orderedList",
        "attrs": {
          "start": 1,
          "type": null
        },
        "content": [
          {
            "type": "listItem",
            "content": [
              {
                "type": "paragraph",
                "content": [
                  {
                    "type": "text",
                    "text": "Add all forces in the positive direction"
                  }
                ]
              }
            ]
          },
          {
            "type": "listItem",
            "content": [
              {
                "type": "paragraph",
                "content": [
                  {
                    "type": "text",
                    "text": "Add all forces in the negative direction"
                  }
                ]
              }
            ]
          },
          {
            "type": "listItem",
            "content": [
              {
                "type": "paragraph",
                "content": [
                  {
                    "type": "text",
                    "text": "Find the difference"
                  }
                ]
              }
            ]
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "type": "heading",
        "attrs": {
          "level": 2
        },
        "content": [
          {
            "type": "text",
            "text": "Mathematical Relationships"
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "type": "heading",
        "attrs": {
          "level": 3
        },
        "content": [
          {
            "type": "text",
            "text": "Rearranging F = ma"
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "type": "paragraph",
        "content": [
          {
            "type": "text",
            "marks": [
              {
                "type": "bold"
              }
            ],
            "text": "To find acceleration:"
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "type": "paragraph",
        "content": [
          {
            "type": "text",
            "text": "$$a = \\frac{F}{m}$$"
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "type": "paragraph",
        "content": [
          {
            "type": "text",
            "marks": [
              {
                "type": "bold"
              }
            ],
            "text": "To find mass:"
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "type": "paragraph",
        "content": [
          {
            "type": "text",
            "text": "$$m = \\frac{F}{a}$$"
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "type": "heading",
        "attrs": {
          "level": 3
        },
        "content": [
          {
            "type": "text",
            "text": "Unit Analysis"
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "type": "paragraph",
        "content": [
          {
            "type": "text",
            "text": "$$1 \\text{ N} = 1 \\text{ kg} \\cdot \\text{m/s}^2$$"
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "type": "paragraph",
        "content": [
          {
            "type": "text",
            "text": "A force of 1 newton gives a 1 kg mass an acceleration of 1 m/s²."
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "type": "heading",
        "attrs": {
          "level": 3
        },
        "content": [
          {
            "type": "text",
            "text": "Weight as a Special Case"
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "type": "paragraph",
        "content": [
          {
            "type": "text",
            "text": "Weight is the force causing free-fall acceleration:"
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "type": "paragraph",
        "content": [
          {
            "type": "text",
            "text": "$$W = mg$$"
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "type": "paragraph",
        "content": [
          {
            "type": "text",
            "text": "This is Newton's Second Law where "
          },
          {
            "type": "text",
            "text": "$a = g$"
          },
          {
            "type": "text",
            "text": "."
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "type": "blockquote",
        "content": [
          {
            "type": "paragraph",
            "content": [
              {
                "type": "text",
                "marks": [
                  {
                    "type": "bold"
                  }
                ],
                "text": "Remember: "
              },
              {
                "type": "text",
                "text": "The F in F = ma is the resultant force. If multiple forces act on an object, you must first find the net force by adding forces in the direction of motion and subtracting forces opposing it."
              }
            ]
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "type": "heading",
        "attrs": {
          "level": 3
        },
        "content": [
          {
            "type": "text",
            "text": "Quick Check"
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "type": "quizEmbed",
        "attrs": {
          "quizId": "check-newtons-second-law"
        }
      },
      {
        "type": "heading",
        "attrs": {
          "level": 2
        },
        "content": [
          {
            "type": "text",
            "text": "Worked Examples"
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "type": "paragraph",
        "content": [
          {
            "type": "text",
            "marks": [
              {
                "type": "bold"
              }
            ],
            "text": "Example 1:"
          },
          {
            "type": "text",
            "text": " A 1200 kg car accelerates at 3 m/s². Calculate the resultant force."
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "type": "paragraph",
        "content": [
          {
            "type": "text",
            "marks": [
              {
                "type": "bold"
              }
            ],
            "text": "Solution:"
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "type": "paragraph",
        "content": [
          {
            "type": "text",
            "text": "$$F = ma = 1200 \\times 3 = 3600 \\text{ N}$$"
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "type": "paragraph",
        "content": [
          {
            "type": "text",
            "marks": [
              {
                "type": "bold"
              }
            ],
            "text": "Example 2:"
          },
          {
            "type": "text",
            "text": " A 500 N force acts on an object, causing an acceleration of 4 m/s². What is the mass?"
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "type": "paragraph",
        "content": [
          {
            "type": "text",
            "marks": [
              {
                "type": "bold"
              }
            ],
            "text": "Solution:"
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "type": "paragraph",
        "content": [
          {
            "type": "text",
            "text": "$$m = \\frac{F}{a} = \\frac{500}{4} = 125 \\text{ kg}$$"
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "type": "paragraph",
        "content": [
          {
            "type": "text",
            "marks": [
              {
                "type": "bold"
              }
            ],
            "text": "Example 3:"
          },
          {
            "type": "text",
            "text": " A 60 kg skater experiences a push of 180 N forward and friction of 30 N backward. Calculate the acceleration."
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "type": "paragraph",
        "content": [
          {
            "type": "text",
            "marks": [
              {
                "type": "bold"
              }
            ],
            "text": "Solution:"
          },
          {
            "type": "text",
            "text": " Resultant force = 180 - 30 = 150 N (forward)"
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "type": "paragraph",
        "content": [
          {
            "type": "text",
            "text": "$$a = \\frac{F}{m} = \\frac{150}{60} = 2.5 \\text{ m/s}^2$$ (forward)\n\n**Example 4:** A car of mass 1500 kg travels at 25 m/s. It brakes and stops in 5 seconds. Calculate:\na) the deceleration\nb) the braking force\n\n**Solution:**\na) Using $a = \\frac{v - u}{t}$:\n$$a = \\frac{0 - 25}{5} = -5 \\text{ m/s}^2$$"
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "type": "paragraph",
        "content": [
          {
            "type": "text",
            "text": "b) Using "
          },
          {
            "type": "text",
            "text": "$F = ma$"
          },
          {
            "type": "text",
            "text": ":"
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "type": "paragraph",
        "content": [
          {
            "type": "text",
            "text": "$$F = 1500 \\times (-5) = -7500 \\text{ N}$$"
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "type": "paragraph",
        "content": [
          {
            "type": "text",
            "text": "The braking force is 7500 N (in the opposite direction to motion)."
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "type": "paragraph",
        "content": [
          {
            "type": "text",
            "marks": [
              {
                "type": "bold"
              }
            ],
            "text": "Example 5:"
          },
          {
            "type": "text",
            "text": " A rocket of mass 50,000 kg produces a thrust of 800,000 N. Its weight is 500,000 N. Calculate the initial acceleration."
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "type": "paragraph",
        "content": [
          {
            "type": "text",
            "marks": [
              {
                "type": "bold"
              }
            ],
            "text": "Solution:"
          },
          {
            "type": "text",
            "text": " Resultant force = Thrust - Weight = 800,000 - 500,000 = 300,000 N (upward)"
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "type": "paragraph",
        "content": [
          {
            "type": "text",
            "text": "$$a = \\frac{F}{m} = \\frac{300,000}{50,000} = 6 \\text{ m/s}^2$$"
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "type": "heading",
        "attrs": {
          "level": 2
        },
        "content": [
          {
            "type": "text",
            "text": "Applications"
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "type": "heading",
        "attrs": {
          "level": 3
        },
        "content": [
          {
            "type": "text",
            "text": "Vehicle Performance"
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "type": "bulletList",
        "content": [
          {
            "type": "listItem",
            "content": [
              {
                "type": "paragraph",
                "content": [
                  {
                    "type": "text",
                    "text": "Engine force determines acceleration"
                  }
                ]
              }
            ]
          },
          {
            "type": "listItem",
            "content": [
              {
                "type": "paragraph",
                "content": [
                  {
                    "type": "text",
                    "text": "Heavier vehicles need more force for the same acceleration"
                  }
                ]
              }
            ]
          },
          {
            "type": "listItem",
            "content": [
              {
                "type": "paragraph",
                "content": [
                  {
                    "type": "text",
                    "text": "Sports cars: high force, low mass = high acceleration"
                  }
                ]
              }
            ]
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "type": "heading",
        "attrs": {
          "level": 3
        },
        "content": [
          {
            "type": "text",
            "text": "Lifts (Elevators)"
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "type": "paragraph",
        "content": [
          {
            "type": "text",
            "text": "When a lift accelerates:"
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "type": "bulletList",
        "content": [
          {
            "type": "listItem",
            "content": [
              {
                "type": "paragraph",
                "content": [
                  {
                    "type": "text",
                    "text": "Accelerating upward: you feel heavier (apparent weight > actual weight)"
                  }
                ]
              }
            ]
          },
          {
            "type": "listItem",
            "content": [
              {
                "type": "paragraph",
                "content": [
                  {
                    "type": "text",
                    "text": "Accelerating downward: you feel lighter (apparent weight < actual weight)"
                  }
                ]
              }
            ]
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "type": "heading",
        "attrs": {
          "level": 3
        },
        "content": [
          {
            "type": "text",
            "text": "Sports"
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "type": "bulletList",
        "content": [
          {
            "type": "listItem",
            "content": [
              {
                "type": "paragraph",
                "content": [
                  {
                    "type": "text",
                    "text": "Sprinters push against starting blocks with large force for high acceleration"
                  }
                ]
              }
            ]
          },
          {
            "type": "listItem",
            "content": [
              {
                "type": "paragraph",
                "content": [
                  {
                    "type": "text",
                    "text": "Tennis rackets apply large force to accelerate the ball"
                  }
                ]
              }
            ]
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "type": "heading",
        "attrs": {
          "level": 3
        },
        "content": [
          {
            "type": "text",
            "text": "Safety Engineering"
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "type": "bulletList",
        "content": [
          {
            "type": "listItem",
            "content": [
              {
                "type": "paragraph",
                "content": [
                  {
                    "type": "text",
                    "text": "Crumple zones increase the time of deceleration, reducing the force"
                  }
                ]
              }
            ]
          },
          {
            "type": "listItem",
            "content": [
              {
                "type": "paragraph",
                "content": [
                  {
                    "type": "text",
                    "text": "Airbags spread the stopping force over a larger area and longer time"
                  }
                ]
              }
            ]
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "type": "heading",
        "attrs": {
          "level": 2
        },
        "content": [
          {
            "type": "text",
            "text": "Free-Body Diagrams"
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "type": "paragraph",
        "content": [
          {
            "type": "text",
            "text": "A "
          },
          {
            "type": "text",
            "marks": [
              {
                "type": "bold"
              }
            ],
            "text": "free-body diagram"
          },
          {
            "type": "text",
            "text": " shows all forces acting on an object."
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "type": "paragraph",
        "content": [
          {
            "type": "text",
            "text": "Steps to draw:"
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "type": "orderedList",
        "attrs": {
          "start": 1,
          "type": null
        },
        "content": [
          {
            "type": "listItem",
            "content": [
              {
                "type": "paragraph",
                "content": [
                  {
                    "type": "text",
                    "text": "Represent the object as a point or simple shape"
                  }
                ]
              }
            ]
          },
          {
            "type": "listItem",
            "content": [
              {
                "type": "paragraph",
                "content": [
                  {
                    "type": "text",
                    "text": "Draw arrows showing each force"
                  }
                ]
              }
            ]
          },
          {
            "type": "listItem",
            "content": [
              {
                "type": "paragraph",
                "content": [
                  {
                    "type": "text",
                    "text": "Arrow length represents force magnitude"
                  }
                ]
              }
            ]
          },
          {
            "type": "listItem",
            "content": [
              {
                "type": "paragraph",
                "content": [
                  {
                    "type": "text",
                    "text": "Arrow direction shows force direction"
                  }
                ]
              }
            ]
          },
          {
            "type": "listItem",
            "content": [
              {
                "type": "paragraph",
                "content": [
                  {
                    "type": "text",
                    "text": "Label each force"
                  }
                ]
              }
            ]
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "type": "imageEmbed",
        "attrs": {
          "src": "https://pub-5ac955cb5fd64af5baaae90d331db66d.r2.dev/images/0926515e-Terminal_velocity.svg",
          "alt": "Free-body diagram of a skydiver showing weight acting downward and air resistance acting upward",
          "title": "",
          "width": "75%",
          "align": "center"
        }
      },
      {
        "type": "blockquote",
        "content": [
          {
            "type": "paragraph",
            "content": [
              {
                "type": "text",
                "marks": [
                  {
                    "type": "bold"
                  }
                ],
                "text": "Common Mistake: "
              },
              {
                "type": "text",
                "text": "Students sometimes use the total of all forces rather than the resultant force in F = ma. For example, if a 500 N engine force pushes a car forward against 200 N of friction, the resultant force is 500 - 200 = 300 N, not 500 N."
              }
            ]
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "type": "heading",
        "attrs": {
          "level": 3
        },
        "content": [
          {
            "type": "text",
            "text": "Quick Check"
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "type": "quizEmbed",
        "attrs": {
          "quizId": "check-newtons-second-law-2"
        }
      },
      {
        "type": "heading",
        "attrs": {
          "level": 2
        },
        "content": [
          {
            "type": "text",
            "text": "Common Misconceptions"
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "type": "orderedList",
        "attrs": {
          "start": 1,
          "type": null
        },
        "content": [
          {
            "type": "listItem",
            "content": [
              {
                "type": "paragraph",
                "content": [
                  {
                    "type": "text",
                    "marks": [
                      {
                        "type": "bold"
                      }
                    ],
                    "text": "\"F = ma means mass causes force\""
                  },
                  {
                    "type": "text",
                    "text": " — No, force causes acceleration. The equation relates all three quantities."
                  }
                ]
              }
            ]
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "type": "orderedList",
        "attrs": {
          "start": 1,
          "type": null
        },
        "content": [
          {
            "type": "listItem",
            "content": [
              {
                "type": "paragraph",
                "content": [
                  {
                    "type": "text",
                    "marks": [
                      {
                        "type": "bold"
                      }
                    ],
                    "text": "\"Heavier objects need more force to move\""
                  },
                  {
                    "type": "text",
                    "text": " — They need more force to achieve the same acceleration. They can still be moved by small forces, just with small acceleration."
                  }
                ]
              }
            ]
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "type": "orderedList",
        "attrs": {
          "start": 1,
          "type": null
        },
        "content": [
          {
            "type": "listItem",
            "content": [
              {
                "type": "paragraph",
                "content": [
                  {
                    "type": "text",
                    "marks": [
                      {
                        "type": "bold"
                      }
                    ],
                    "text": "\"Constant force means constant velocity\""
                  },
                  {
                    "type": "text",
                    "text": " — No! Constant force means constant acceleration (velocity keeps changing)."
                  }
                ]
              }
            ]
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "type": "orderedList",
        "attrs": {
          "start": 1,
          "type": null
        },
        "content": [
          {
            "type": "listItem",
            "content": [
              {
                "type": "paragraph",
                "content": [
                  {
                    "type": "text",
                    "marks": [
                      {
                        "type": "bold"
                      }
                    ],
                    "text": "\"If something is moving, there must be a net force\""
                  },
                  {
                    "type": "text",
                    "text": " — Not necessarily. Constant velocity requires zero net force (First Law)."
                  }
                ]
              }
            ]
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "type": "orderedList",
        "attrs": {
          "start": 1,
          "type": null
        },
        "content": [
          {
            "type": "listItem",
            "content": [
              {
                "type": "paragraph",
                "content": [
                  {
                    "type": "text",
                    "marks": [
                      {
                        "type": "bold"
                      }
                    ],
                    "text": "\"You can use any force in F = ma\""
                  },
                  {
                    "type": "text",
                    "text": " — You must use the resultant (net) force, not individual forces."
                  }
                ]
              }
            ]
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "type": "heading",
        "attrs": {
          "level": 3
        },
        "content": [
          {
            "type": "text",
            "text": "Knowledge Check"
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "type": "quizEmbed",
        "attrs": {
          "quizId": "quiz-newtons-second-law"
        }
      },
      {
        "type": "heading",
        "attrs": {
          "level": 3
        },
        "content": [
          {
            "type": "text",
            "text": "Lesson Complete - Test Yourself"
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "type": "quizEmbed",
        "attrs": {
          "quizId": "practice-newtons-second-law"
        }
      },
      {
        "type": "heading",
        "attrs": {
          "level": 2
        },
        "content": [
          {
            "type": "text",
            "text": "Summary"
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "type": "bulletList",
        "content": [
          {
            "type": "listItem",
            "content": [
              {
                "type": "paragraph",
                "content": [
                  {
                    "type": "text",
                    "text": "Newton's Second Law: "
                  },
                  {
                    "type": "text",
                    "text": "$F = ma$"
                  }
                ]
              }
            ]
          },
          {
            "type": "listItem",
            "content": [
              {
                "type": "paragraph",
                "content": [
                  {
                    "type": "text",
                    "text": "Acceleration is proportional to resultant force"
                  }
                ]
              }
            ]
          },
          {
            "type": "listItem",
            "content": [
              {
                "type": "paragraph",
                "content": [
                  {
                    "type": "text",
                    "text": "Acceleration is inversely proportional to mass"
                  }
                ]
              }
            ]
          },
          {
            "type": "listItem",
            "content": [
              {
                "type": "paragraph",
                "content": [
                  {
                    "type": "text",
                    "text": "Acceleration is in the direction of the resultant force"
                  }
                ]
              }
            ]
          },
          {
            "type": "listItem",
            "content": [
              {
                "type": "paragraph",
                "content": [
                  {
                    "type": "text",
                    "text": "Always use the resultant (net) force in calculations"
                  }
                ]
              }
            ]
          },
          {
            "type": "listItem",
            "content": [
              {
                "type": "paragraph",
                "content": [
                  {
                    "type": "text",
                    "text": "Units: Force (N), Mass (kg), Acceleration (m/s²)"
                  }
                ]
              }
            ]
          },
          {
            "type": "listItem",
            "content": [
              {
                "type": "paragraph",
                "content": [
                  {
                    "type": "text",
                    "text": "1 N = 1 kg·m/s²"
                  }
                ]
              }
            ]
          },
          {
            "type": "listItem",
            "content": [
              {
                "type": "paragraph",
                "content": [
                  {
                    "type": "text",
                    "text": "Free-body diagrams help identify all forces acting on an object"
                  }
                ]
              }
            ]
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "type": "paragraph",
        "content": [
          {
            "type": "text",
            "text": "Explore Newton's laws of motion — see all three laws demonstrated with interactive examples using the interactive visualisation below."
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "type": "visualizationEmbed",
        "attrs": {
          "visualizationId": "phys-newtons-laws-demo",
          "displayMode": "expanded",
          "customHeight": 500
        }
      },
      {
        "type": "orderedList",
        "attrs": {
          "start": 1,
          "type": null
        },
        "content": [
          {
            "type": "listItem",
            "content": [
              {
                "type": "paragraph",
                "content": [
                  {
                    "type": "text",
                    "marks": [
                      {
                        "type": "bold"
                      }
                    ],
                    "text": "500,000 N"
                  },
                  {
                    "type": "text",
                    "text": " — Weight "
                  },
                  {
                    "type": "text",
                    "text": "$= 20{,}000 \\times 10 = 200{,}000$"
                  },
                  {
                    "type": "text",
                    "text": " N; resultant "
                  },
                  {
                    "type": "text",
                    "text": "$= ma = 20{,}000 \\times 15 = 300{,}000$"
                  },
                  {
                    "type": "text",
                    "text": " N; thrust "
                  },
                  {
                    "type": "text",
                    "text": "$= 200{,}000 + 300{,}000 = 500{,}000$"
                  },
                  {
                    "type": "text",
                    "text": " N (calculation)"
                  }
                ]
              }
            ]
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "type": "paragraph"
      }
    ]
  }
}